This paper presents $2$ independent proofs demonstrating that the Diophantine equation $a^2+ab+b^2=7^n$ possesses $2$ distinct proofs showing that the set of whole number solutions to the equation $a^2+ab+b^2=7^n$ is given by
$$
\begin{aligned}
\left\{
\begin{pmatrix}
3^{k} & 2 \cdot 3^{j} \\
2 \cdot 3^{i} & 2 \cdot 3^{h}
\end{pmatrix}
\right\}
\end{aligned}
$$
where $2 \leq i < j \leq k = n$.
